  - **Hypothesis A (final checkpoint better than `best/`): rejected.**
    `checkpoint-3810/` at conf 0.5 averages **mAP@0.5 = 0.0000**
    across the 5 PDFs (vs `best/`'s 0.0337). It lost the only
    correct prediction `best/` had on generic3. So the HF
    best-epoch picker, even though it was choosing from a 0.0002-0.0010
    noise band, still ended up at a *better* state than the
    final-epoch state. Disabling `load_best_model_at_end` is
    therefore not a useful fix.
  - **Hypothesis B (confidence calibration is off): rejected.**
    Re-evaluating `best/` at conf 0.1 surfaced one additional correct
    prediction (makayla1, IoU 0.72) and three confident-but-wrong
    predictions. Average mAP 0.0337 -> 0.0594. Dropping further to
    conf 0.05 gave **identical** results -- the model has zero
    additional proposals in the [0.05, 0.1) confidence band on any
    PDF. So there is no meaningful calibration uplift to recover.
  - **Diagnosis: mode collapse to 1 prediction per page.** Across
    all 15 runs (3 thresholds x 5 PDFs), the model produces *exactly
    1 prediction per page* at every threshold below 0.1, regardless
    of how many panels are actually there (GT counts: 6, 7, 8, 3,
    4). TATR's underlying DETR has 20 object queries (v6 retrain
    used `--num-queries 20`), but this model effectively fires only
    one. The most damning datapoint: on
    `Electrical_Takeoff_page7.pdf` (v4 = 1.00 mAP, the "easiest"
    of the 5), v6 produces a single wrong box. This is classic DETR
    mode collapse -- a known failure mode when training data is
    small or imbalanced, or when classification loss dominates bbox
    loss during early training.
