- **v6 root-cause investigation -- DONE 2026-05-14** -- After the
  diagnostic sweep ruled out cheap inference-time fixes, the
  training-time forensics (`train.log`, `trainer_state.json`,
  `training_args.bin`) plus a side-by-side hyperparameter diff
  against v4 plus a synthetic-positional-bias check together
  identify the v6 **training recipe** as the cause of the mode
  collapse. The architecture and the expanded dataset are not at
  fault. The model is salvageable with a recipe-fix retrain.
  - **Training dynamics (from `trainer_state.json`)**:
    - `best_global_step = 762` (epoch 6 of 30). The "best"
      checkpoint that ended up at `models_v6/best/` was selected
      from epoch 6 -- *before* the backbone unfreeze at epoch 10.
    - `best_metric = 0.001`. The highest eval `map_50` observed
      during the entire 30-epoch run on the v6 VAL split (same
      distribution as training) was 0.001. The model never crossed
      even a 0.01 mAP threshold on its own validation set.
    - Training loss declined from 8.6 -> 4.9 (pre-unfreeze) -> 3.9
      (immediate post-unfreeze) -> 3.3 (plateau through end of
      training). DETR fine-tuning typically reaches ~1-2; v6
      plateaued at ~3.3.
    - Grad norms spiked late: ~20 pre-unfreeze, 50-120 in the
      middle, 191 at the final step. With `max_grad_norm=0.1`
      hard-coded in the script (see below), gradients of magnitude
      191 are clipped by ~1900x -- the model effectively cannot
      take big optimization steps even when it wants to.
  - **Hyperparameter diff (v4 worked, mAP 0.47; v6 failed, mAP 0.03)**:
    | arg | v4 | v6 | direction |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | `learning_rate` | 1e-5 | **5e-6** | v6 is half v4 |
    | `warmup_steps` | 50 | 100 | v6 2x |
    | `weight_decay` | 0.01 | **0.05** | v6 5x |
    | `per_device_train_batch_size` | 1 | 2 | v6 2x |
    | `num_train_epochs` | 15 | 30 | v6 2x |
    | `lr_scheduler_type` | linear | linear | same |
    | `max_grad_norm` | 0.1 | 0.1 | same (hard-coded in script) |
    | `--freeze-backbone-epochs` | none | ~9 epochs frozen | new |
    | `--num-queries` | 15 (default) | 20 | new (5 random-init queries) |
    | `--hard-negatives-dir` | none | used | new (per earlier audit: redundant) |
    | Dataset | 28 real images | 48 real + 250 synthetic = 298 | expanded |
  - **Why this combination starves the backbone**: v6 freezes the
    backbone for the first 30% of training (1270 steps). When the
    callback unfreezes the backbone at step 1270, the LR scheduler
    is linear-decay from peak 5e-6 and has already reached 3.42e-6
    (67% of peak, 33% decayed). From there it linearly decays to
    1.35e-9 over the remaining 2540 steps. So the backbone -- the
    part of the model that has to adapt PubTables-1M features to
    engineering drawings -- gets at most ~2540 steps under an LR
    of 3.4e-6 -> 0, with all gradients clipped to norm 0.1. By
    contrast, v4's backbone was *never* frozen and trained the full
    run under LR 1e-5 -> 0. v6's backbone effectively never trained.
  - **Symptom matches the cause**: when the backbone never adapts,
    the model can't learn proper localization. It falls back on a
    degenerate solution -- predict no-object for most queries, and
    one box near the spatial centroid of training panel positions.
    The combined v6 dataset has mean centroid `(cx=0.501, cy=0.482)`
    -- dead-center of the page -- which is exactly where v6's
    single box landed on `D.pdf` during the conf-0.05 viz run
    (overlay at
    `~/Documents/TableAnnotations/baselines/v6_best_conf005_viz_2026-05-14/D/ml/magenta_overlays/`).
  - **Synthetic positional bias check: REFUTED**. Computed
    normalized panel-center distributions on the v6 dataset
    (real n=144, synthetic n=677):
    - Real `cx`: mean 0.47, stdev 0.21, range 0.16-0.79;
      `cy`: mean 0.42, stdev 0.21, range 0.11-0.86.
    - Synthetic `cx`: mean 0.51, stdev 0.22, range 0.09-0.90;
      `cy`: mean 0.50, stdev 0.21, range 0.07-0.89.
    Synthetic placements cover 80%+ of page width and 82%+ of page
    height with stdev essentially identical to real. The
    cut-and-paste synthesis did *not* introduce a positional
    cluster. So the v6 dataset is usable as-is; the recipe is
    what failed it.
  - **Proposed v7 recipe** (single-variable change vs v4: only the
    dataset):
    ```
    --learning-rate 1e-5            (was 5e-6)
    --warmup-steps 50               (was 100)
    --weight-decay 0.01             (was 0.05)
    --epochs 15                     (was 30; extra epochs didn't help)
